<%# Health, on HEALTH.md's thesis: the assistant's job here is logistics and
    comprehension, never diagnosis. Says so early, because a health page in
    an AI product has to name its scope line before it can be trusted. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    What an assistant actually does for someone's health is mostly not
    medical. They make sure the checkup gets booked. They remember when the
    last one was. They keep the insurance paperwork findable, sit on hold
    with the insurer, and make sure you walk into the appointment with your
    questions written down instead of remembering them in the car park
    afterwards.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's the job here. This section will never tell you what's wrong with
    you — it does preparation and plain language, so you arrive ready and
    leave understanding what you were told.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("health") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you see</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "You're due") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The checks that have quietly gone too long — the dentist, the eye
        test, the screening nobody scheduled — surfaced before they become a
        thing you feel guilty about. For everyone in the house, including
        the ones with four legs.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "You're booked") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The quieter half, and the one that matters more. An assistant that
        can only nag adds anxiety; this one can also say <em>handled</em>,
        and mean it, so you can stop carrying it around.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The paperwork, findable") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Insurance cards, plan documents, benefit summaries, the letter about
        the claim. Filed, searchable inside, and summarised — so "what does
        my plan actually cover for this" has an answer that takes a minute.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Medications and allergies") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Kept in one place, because the form at every new clinic asks for
        exactly this list and nobody has ever had it to hand.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The line it won't cross</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Preparation and comprehension, never diagnosis. It will explain what the
    words on your results letter mean and list the questions worth asking
    your doctor about them. It will not tell you what they indicate, and it
    will not suggest a treatment. Where something is genuinely unclear it
    says so rather than smoothing over it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    This is a scope line rather than a legal disclaimer, and the difference
    shows in the output: the app is trying to make you a better-informed
    patient, not to replace the appointment.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">No step counting</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    You won't find sleep graphs or heart-rate trends here. Not because
    they're worthless, but because every honest route to that data is either
    shut off, locked to a phone, or a separate integration with a company
    per device — and a chart with nothing to say is worse than no chart. The
    durable, universally useful health assets turned out to be appointments
    and paperwork, so those are what this section is built around.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Everyone in the house</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Health is rarely only yours. The parent you've started caring for, the
    child whose immunisations you track, the dog whose vaccination is due —
    all of them generate exactly this kind of work, and all of them are
    handled here rather than in a separate app each. Pets get their own
    page, their own record, and their own due-soon list.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Who can see it</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Nobody but you, unless you say so. Health is the area where that default
    matters most, and where the app is most careful never to guess: sharing
    a diagnosis with a spouse, or a child's records between two parents, is
    something you switch on per item or per area and can revoke at any time.
  </p>
</section>
